//! The List module: a **genuinely growable** array of `s64`, backed by the heap.
//!
//! The fifth module, and the one `Array` could not be. ADR-0105 shipped a fixed-capacity buffer and named three
//! refusals as the reason; ADR-0106 lifted the first of them, so this exists.
//!
//! # Why this is a new module rather than a rewrite of `Array`
//!
//! The two have genuinely different contracts, and collapsing them would hide that. An `Array(s64)` needs **no
//! cleanup** — its storage is inline, so a caller can forget about it — while an `List(s64)` **owns heap memory**
//! and a caller must call `free_data`. That is not a detail: there are no destructors in Jairs, which is a
//! design value rather than a missing feature, so ownership is something a caller reads in a type's name and
//! docs or does not learn at all.
//!
//! An `Array(s64)` is also the better choice when a bound is known — no allocation, no failure mode, no
//! cleanup. Replacing it would have traded a simpler thing for a more capable one, which is not an improvement
//! when both are cheap to keep.
//!
//! # Why `Int_`, still
//!
//! **Cross-file parameterised structs are deferred** (E0269, ADR-0085 §5), so a `struct($T)` here would be
//! unusable by every importer — exactly the discovery that shaped `Array`. And **inference through a
//! parameterised struct** is deferred too, so even inside one file the routines could not be generic. Typed
//! allocation lifted the *storage* blocker; these two remain, and they are what stand between this and a real
//! `List($T)`.
//!
//! # Why growth doubles
//!
//! `n` pushes cost `O(n)` amortised, which is the property that makes a growable array worth having. A fixed
//! increment makes it `O(n²)` — a bug wearing a policy's clothes. The first allocation is **4** elements: 1
//! would mean three reallocations before a small list settles, and 16 would make a list that stays small hold
//! 128 unused bytes.
//!
//! # Why a failed allocation is `false` and not a trap
//!
//! ADR-0058 §4's line is that a trap is for a **program** error. Running out of memory is not one — the program
//! is correct and the machine said no — so aborting the process would take away the caller's only chance to
//! recover. `push` returns `false`, which is what it already returned when `Array(s64)` was full: a caller can do
//! nothing different about the two reasons, so one answer serves both.
//!
//! # Composing with the rest of the library
//!
//! `elements(a)` hands out a `[]s64` **over the used prefix**, so a list's contents go straight to `Sort` or to
//! anything else taking a view. That was this module's closing gap when it shipped: a slice takes an *array*, so
//! nothing could turn a pointer and a count into a view, and a growable array and a sorting routine sat side by
//! side unable to be combined. ADR-0109's `view` intrinsic closed it.
//!
//! # Why this module imports `Basic`
//!
//! It calls `malloc` and `free`, so it needs them — and **needing them is not the same as having them**. Without
//! the import the names still *resolved*, because ADR-0014 merges the root file's imports flatly, so the module
//! type-checked and then failed at run time with `no routine for file 2 proc 0`: a signature had crossed the
//! boundary and a body had not. A module must import what it uses, and this is the first module that uses
//! anything.

#import "Basic";

/// A count, a capacity, and heap storage: the list's elements are `data[0 .. count)`.
///
/// **The caller owns `data`.** `free_data` releases it, and nothing else will — there are no destructors. A list
/// that is dropped without `free_data` leaks, which is the honest cost of explicit memory and the reason the
/// type's name says `List` rather than pretending to be a value.
///
/// `data` is null until the first `push`, so declaring a list allocates nothing. That makes an unused list free
/// and makes `free_data` safe to call on one that never grew.
List :: struct($T) {
    /// The storage, or null before the first `push`. Typed via `typed` (ADR-0106 §1), because an allocator
    /// returns `*u8` and a general pointer cast is refused.
    data: *T;
    /// How many elements are in use.
    count: s64;
    /// How many the current allocation holds. Zero exactly when `data` is null.
    capacity: s64;
}

/// The capacity of a list's first allocation.
///
/// Exported so a caller can reason about the first `push`'s cost, and because a number that governs behaviour
/// should be nameable rather than buried in a routine.
FIRST_CAPACITY :: 4;

/// Appends `v`, answering `false` when the allocation it needed failed.
///
/// **`false` means "out of memory"**, which is the only way this can fail — a list has no fixed bound. A caller
/// can do nothing different about that than about `Array(s64)`'s "full", which is why the signature is the same.
///
/// Amortised `O(1)`: the capacity doubles, so `n` pushes copy `O(n)` elements in total.
push :: (a: *List(s64), v: s64) -> bool {
    if a.count >= a.capacity {
        if !grow(a) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    (a.data + a.count).* = v;
    a.count = a.count + 1;
    return true;
}

/// Removes and returns the last element, plus whether there was one.
///
/// Two return values (ADR-0008) rather than a sentinel, for the reason `Int_Array.pop` gives: every `s64` is a
/// legitimate element, so no value could mean "empty" without excluding it from the list.
///
/// **The capacity is not reduced.** A caller who pops and pushes would otherwise thrash the allocator, and
/// nothing has asked for the memory back — `free_data` is how a caller says they are finished.
pop :: (a: *List(s64)) -> (s64, bool) {
    if a.count == 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    a.count = a.count - 1;
    return (a.data + a.count).*, true;
}

/// The element at `index`, plus whether the index was in the **used** range.
///
/// Bounded on `count`, not `capacity`: the slots between them hold whatever the allocator gave back, which is
/// genuinely undefined rather than merely unused. That is a sharper reason than `Array(s64)`'s — there the extra
/// slots were zeroed and therefore only *indistinguishable* from real elements — and it is why this bound is not
/// a matter of taste.
get :: (a: *List(s64), index: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    if index < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    if index >= a.count {
        return 0, false;
    }
    return (a.data + index).*, true;
}

/// Replaces the element at `index`, answering `false` when the index is outside the used range.
///
/// It will not extend the list — `set(a, a.count, v)` is `false`, not a push — because growth through
/// assignment would make the length depend on which indices a caller happened to touch.
set :: (a: *List(s64), index: s64, v: s64) -> bool {
    if index < 0 {
        return false;
    }
    if index >= a.count {
        return false;
    }
    (a.data + index).* = v;
    return true;
}

/// Forgets the elements, keeping the allocation.
///
/// Deliberately **not** the same as `free_data`: reusing a buffer a caller has already paid for is a real thing
/// to want, and naming both makes the choice visible. A reader who wants the memory back wants `free_data`.
clear :: (a: *List(s64)) {
    a.count = 0;
}

/// Releases the storage and empties the list.
///
/// **This is the routine a caller must not forget.** There are no destructors, so nothing else will free the
/// memory. Safe to call on a list that never grew (`data` is null and `free` of null is a no-op) and safe to
/// call twice, because it resets `data` — a second call frees nothing rather than freeing again.
free_data :: (a: *List(s64)) {
    if a.data != null {
        free(untyped(a.data));
    }
    a.data = null;
    a.count = 0;
    a.capacity = 0;
}

/// Whether the list holds nothing.
is_empty :: (a: *List(s64)) -> bool {
    return a.count == 0;
}

/// A view over the list's **used prefix** — `[]s64` of exactly `count` elements.
///
/// This is what makes a list compose: `sort_ints(elements(*l))` sorts it in place, because a view is a *window*
/// onto the same storage rather than a copy (ADR-0044). Anything taking a `[]s64` takes a list's contents.
///
/// **`count`, not `capacity`.** The slots between them hold whatever the allocator returned, so a view over the
/// capacity would hand out genuinely undefined elements — the same reason `get` bounds on `count`.
///
/// **The view is invalidated by anything that reallocates.** A `push` that grows moves the storage, so a view
/// taken before it points at freed memory; `free_data` invalidates it outright. That is the ordinary consequence
/// of a view being a window and of memory being explicit, and it is stated here because nothing enforces it —
/// there is no borrow checker, which is a design position rather than an omission.
///
/// A view over an **empty** list is `view(null, 0)`, which is well-formed: a zero-count view is never indexed,
/// and every routine that takes one loops `while i < count`.
elements :: (a: *List(s64)) -> []s64 {
    return view(a.data, a.count);
}

#scope_module

/// Doubles the capacity, copying the elements across; `false` when the allocation failed.
///
/// Module-private because it is an implementation detail of `push` — a caller who called it directly would be
/// reasoning about capacity, which is precisely what a growable array exists to stop them doing.
///
/// **Allocate, copy, free** rather than `realloc`. The platform's `realloc` may extend in place, and using it
/// would make growth depend on an allocator behaviour the comptime VM does not model — so the two engines could
/// differ in *timing*. Not in results, but a divergence worth not introducing casually in a project whose whole
/// test strategy is comparing them.
grow :: (a: *List(s64)) -> bool {
    wanted := FIRST_CAPACITY;
    if a.capacity > 0 {
        wanted = a.capacity * 2;
    }
    raw := malloc(wanted * size_of(s64));
    if raw == null {
        return false;
    }
    fresh := typed(s64, raw);
    i := 0;
    while i < a.count {
        (fresh + i).* = (a.data + i).*;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if a.data != null {
        free(untyped(a.data));
    }
    a.data = fresh;
    a.capacity = wanted;
    return true;
}
